  "coreDrivers": {
    "policies": "The scenario doesn't follow policies: PDP8 plans only 2.8 of 11 GW of LNG additions. Rail electrification and exogenous modal shift follows local policy. Power operating rules are relaxed: early coal decommissioning and flexible operation from 2030.",
    "emissionsTargets": "Same National Climate Change Strategy caps as NZ (101 Mt energy + 20 Mt process in 2050); identical by design because Green Growth assumes change in economy, not in scenario ambition.",
    "technologyCosts": "Least-cost optimisation as in NZ, but lower demand means no nuclear, LNG at 19 GW and only ~50% of RE potential used; total system cost 13% below BSL.",
    "investmentChange": "not applicable.",
    "macroeconomicDrivers": "Differentiating driver with NZ scenario: same 6.5% GDP growth but industry falls to 30% and services rise to 60% of GDP by 2050, with growth in high-value manufacturing. Industrial energy service demand grows 4.2-fold (vs 5.6 in BSL) and electricity demand reaches only ~1,010 TWh.",
    "behavioralShifts": "Inherits NZ modal shift to rail/public transport and demand flexibility.",
    "otherDrivers": "Renewable potential: 272 GW land solar as in NZ, but much less binding because demand is lower."
  },
  "dependencies": [
